Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm
Cardiovascular
Pulsatile abdominal mass, Severe abdominal/back pain, Aortic dilation >3cm

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-001
Reason for Visit: Acute onset deep abdominal pain with reported palpable mass requiring urgent after-hours physical assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified new onset deep, persistent abdominal pain with a pulsating sensation, a high-risk indicator for an expanding aneurysm. Assessment could not be safely delayed until the routine in-hours period due to the risk of imminent rupture and catastrophic hemorrhage, necessitating urgent clinical evaluation to determine immediate Emergency Department transfer versus outpatient fast-track imaging.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a constant, deep ache in my stomach and back, and I can feel a pulse beating hard in my belly."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began 12 hours ago with a deep, dull, persistent abdominal pain radiating to the lower back. Pain intensity has steadily increased over the evening. Patient reports a new, distinct pulsatile sensation in the mid-abdomen. Denies frank syncope, though reports transient presyncope upon standing. KEY SYMPTOMS: Pulsatile abdominal mass. Deep dull persistent abdominal or back pain. Syncope or presyncope. Tachycardia.
Functional Status: Independent, currently resting supine due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives with spouse who is highly anxious regarding the sudden onset of symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97 percent | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Apprehensive, guarding abdomen, pale but peripherally warm.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardic regular rhythm, no murmurs. Abdomen: Tender to deep palpation centrally. Distinct pulsatile abdominal mass palpated supra-umbilically. Aortic bruit auscultated over the abdomen. No mottling of lower extremities. No Grey Turner's or Cullen's sign in flanks. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Pulsatile abdominal mass, Severe abdominal/back pain, Aortic dilation >3cm (presumed clinically, pending imaging).
Environmental Observations: Clear pathways, well-lit, telephone accessible.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against bedside blister pack. Adherent to daily antihypertensives.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Renal Colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of severe sudden onset unilateral flank pain and negative costovertebral angle tenderness.

Diverticulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, localized left lower quadrant peritonism, and unaltered bowel habits.

Musculoskeletal Back Pain - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain unaffected by movement, presence of pulsatile abdominal mass and bruit.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Presentation features a new, deep, persistent abdominal and back pain coupled with a palpable pulsatile abdominal mass and borderline tachycardia. This clinical picture is highly suspicious for a symptomatic or rapidly expanding abdominal aortic aneurysm. A delay in assessment until the next in-hours period poses an unacceptable risk of acute rupture, exsanguination, and death. Urgent home assessment was strictly required to palpate the abdomen, formally assess hemodynamic stability, and facilitate immediate, safe escalation of care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Nil by mouth ordered. Immediate transfer to Emergency Department via ambulance for urgent CT angiography and vascular surgery review.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain stable blood pressure during transport, patient instructed to remain strictly supine and practice slow, controlled diaphragmatic breathing. The reward is a measurable reduction in acute anxiety and a stabilized heart rate to reduce aortic sheer stress.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Immediate Emergency Department transfer. Routine General Practice review post-discharge.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched from residence. If sudden severe tearing pain, loss of consciousness, or profound sweating occurs prior to arrival, spouse instructed to commence basic life support if patient becomes unresponsive.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal imaging investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Symptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysms carry a high risk of spontaneous rupture. Immediate surgical evaluation is mandatory.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Immediate ambulance transfer initiated. Do not eat or drink anything. Do not stand or walk.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The main blood vessel in the abdomen may be dangerously stretched. Hospital imaging is required to measure it accurately and plan emergency management.
Actionable Steps: Remain perfectly still on the bed. Engage in slow breathing to prevent blood pressure spikes while awaiting paramedics.

